    "body": "- Key criteria: simplicity, not adding new services like a db.\n- It would be great if we can allow users to like a form, and show a total count, allowing filter by 'popularity'.\n- Simply create a stats.json file in bucket root.\n- Pull, update, push when a user likes (via go API endpoint). Map form id to like count.\n- Race conditions possible, but its low traffic and doesn't matter much. Optimising on simplicity.\n- Display the likes on the metadata cards, if stats.json manages to load at first load (good cache policy also worthwhile via s3 for this file).\n\n**Avoiding spamming the like button**\n\n- A basic local storage approach per xlsform id will do.\n- Prevent second click after the first.\n- Obviously this can be abused, but its a community site and trust is key.\n\nAlso, just because, we could probably add a small in-memory rate limit:\n```go\nif tooManyLikesFromIP(r.RemoteAddr) {\n   http.Error(w, \"Rate limit exceeded\", 429)\n   return\n}\n```\n\n**Alternatives considered**:\n\n- External click tracking options.\n- Dynamodb + lambda on top of S3.\n- One file per like in S3, then scheduled aggregation (more atomic).\n- Also considered logging S3 requests, aggregating downloads of forms, then sorting by 'popularity' of download instead of likes. Prone to bots, programmatic usage skew, etc.",
